## Four traps that cost real hours (2026-08-24)

Each of these fails SILENTLY: no exception, no error in `drain_events()`, just a
wrong result you only catch by looking at pixels. Read this section before any
task that types into a page, animates a cursor, or resolves a profile port.

### 1. Rich-text editors reset the caret to 0 after a programmatic insert

Quill (LinkedIn's composer, Notion-likes, many comment boxes) does NOT keep the
caret where `Input.insertText` left it. The next thing you type lands at the TOP
of the document. A LinkedIn post came out as `@WeMakeDevsMetropolis Healthcare
publishes...` because of this, twice, before it was diagnosed.

Always re-anchor the caret before typing after any bulk insert:

```python
cdp('Input.dispatchKeyEvent', type='rawKeyDown', key='End', code='End',
    windowsVirtualKeyCode=35, commands=['moveToEndOfDocument'])
cdp('Input.dispatchKeyEvent', type='keyUp', key='End', code='End',
    windowsVirtualKeyCode=35)
```

`commands=[...]` is the editing-command channel and is also the ONLY reliable
clear: `modifiers=4` + `key='a'` does not select-all in these editors. Use
`commands=['selectAll']` then `commands=['deleteBackward']`. Getting this wrong
appends instead of replacing, which silently doubles the text and can trip a
character limit.

### 2. Mention/@ typeahead only fires on genuine per-character key events

`Input.insertText` and `type_text()` do not open the autocomplete. No dropdown
means no real tag, just grey plain text that looks tagged in a screenshot.

```python
for ch in "@WeMakeDevs":
    cdp('Input.dispatchKeyEvent', type='keyDown', text=ch, unmodifiedText=ch, key=ch)
    cdp('Input.dispatchKeyEvent', type='keyUp', key=ch)
    time.sleep(0.13)
```

If the dropdown still does not appear, delete the last character and retype it;
that re-triggers the query. Then click the suggestion and CONFIRM the inserted
mention renders bold before continuing. Multiple companies share a name, so read
the subtitle line ("Software Development" vs "Marketing Services") before
clicking.

### 3. Occluded windows throttle setTimeout and rAF to about 1Hz

`bh-multi` launches Brave with `open -g`, so the window is backgrounded and
timers are throttled hard. Measured on this machine: twenty chained 100ms
in-page sleeps took over 110 seconds instead of 2. Any gesture, animation, or
`scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})` built on in-page timing appears to hang,
and three capture agents were killed by watchdogs before this was understood.

- Never `await` a chain of in-page sleeps. Own the clock in the driver: one
  `js()` call per animation step, with the driver sleeping between steps.
- CSS keyframe animations do not advance between screenshots. Drive opacity and
  transform frame by frame instead.
- `cdp('Page.setWebLifecycleState', state='active')` helps but does not fully
  restore timer speed. Do not rely on it alone.

Working driver-side implementation: `~/Desktop/bright/demo-videos/scripts/shoot.py`
plus `cursor_overlay.js` (visible synthetic pointer, since a CDP screenshot never
contains the real OS pointer and synthetic clicks otherwise look like the page
changing by itself).

### 4. `bh-multi run <name>` can disagree with `bhn` about the port

`bh-multi` derives a port by hashing the profile name, but a profile that claimed
the base port first is registered elsewhere. `deepsurge` was live on 9301 while
`bh-multi run deepsurge` and `gform --profile deepsurge` both looked at 9396 and
reported "Not running".

Resolve the port from the running registry, then talk to the harness directly:

```bash
PORT=$(bhn ls --running | awk '$1=="<profile>"{print $2}')
WS=$(curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:$PORT/json/version" | jq -r .webSocketDebuggerUrl)
BU_NAME=<profile> BU_CDP_WS="$WS" browser-harness -c "<code>"
```

### Bonus: the `js()` escaping trap

The harness evaluates its argument as a PYTHON string first. A literal `\n`
inside a JSON payload embedded in `js('''...''')` becomes a real newline and
tears the JS string in half, which surfaces as `js()` returning `None` rather
than as an error. Send any non-trivial payload as base64 and decode it inside
the page:

```python
b = base64.b64encode(text.encode()).decode()
js(f'(function(){{var v=decodeURIComponent(escape(atob("{b}")));...}})()')
```

### Verification rule for all of the above

These failures are invisible to DOM reads: the text is present, just in the
wrong place, or the tag is present, just not a real tag. After any typing,
mention, or gesture task, take ONE screenshot and actually look at it before
reporting success. This is the documented exception to the screenshots-off
default.
# One browser profile for research: `deepsurge`

All reading, searching, and general browsing happens in the **`deepsurge`** profile.
One window, many tabs. Never a fresh profile per query, per subagent, or per topic.

## The rule

```bash
bhn deepsurge https://example.com     # open / navigate the research window
bhn deepsurge open https://other.com  # additional tab in the SAME window
bhn deepsurge tabs                    # what is already open
bhn deepsurge use <target-id-or-url>  # switch which tab commands act on
bhn deepsurge state compact           # read the focused tab
```

`bhn deep` resolves to `deepsurge` by prefix, so the short form is fine.

Subagents inherit this. A research fan-out of eight agents uses **one** profile and
opens eight tabs, not eight profiles. Tell every spawned research agent which profile
to use, in the prompt, by name.

## Runs on the Mac mini by default

Research and screenshot work runs on the Mac mini over Tailscale, not the laptop, so
the laptop carries no browser load: `minibhn <bhn args...>` forwards to `bhn` on the
mini, `minishot URL OUT WIDTH HEIGHT` shoots there and copies the PNG back. Use these
instead of local `bhn`/`shot` unless a task needs the laptop's signed-in session.

The mini's single profile is also named `deepsurge`, but it is a separate, signed-out
browser profile, not signed in to anything by default. If a task needs a signed-in
session there (e.g. chatgpt.com), the user signs in by hand on the mini once; do not
try to copy cookies or session files from the laptop to make it signed in. For any
task that depends on the laptop's signed-in `deepsurge` (email, paywalled/gated
content, reply/subscribe/export from an existing session), use the laptop's local
`bhn deepsurge`, not `minibhn`.

## Why this profile

`deepsurge` is signed in, including email. That is not incidental, it is the point:
a logged-in session reads paywalled and gated pages, keeps result quality consistent,
and makes follow-on automation (reply, subscribe, export, fill a form) possible from
the same tab the research is already in. A throwaway profile is signed out, gets more
bot checks, and throws the session away when the query ends.

## Creating profiles

A new profile now requires an explicit flag, because an unrecognized name used to
mint one silently and pop a second Brave window with a blank tab:

```bash
bhn --new <name>            # the only way to create one
BHN_ALLOW_NEW_PROFILE=1     # env equivalent, for scripted QA setup
```

An unknown name without `--new` exits 2 and prints near matches. If that error
appears during research, the answer is `bhn deepsurge`, not `--new`.

Create a separate profile only for a genuine identity boundary:

- per-account QA and E2E fixtures, where a clean signed-out state is the test
- a second logged-in identity that must not share cookies with `deepsurge`
- an untrusted or red-team page that should not touch a signed-in session

Delete these when the task ends. `bhn ls` lists every profile, `bhn stop-all` closes
running windows. A profile list that has grown a tail of one-off research names means
this rule was skipped.

## Close the tabs you opened

One profile with thirty abandoned source tabs is the same defect as thirty
profiles, just cheaper to miss. Reading a source is a step, not a bookmark: when
the task that opened the tabs is done, sweep them.

```bash
bhn deepsurge sweep                 # tabs this run opened
bhn sweep --all --marked            # every running profile, incl. 🟢-marked tabs
bhn deepsurge sweep --dry-run       # list first
```

A sweep closes only tabs the harness opened or tabs carrying the 🟢 agent marker,
so a page the user opened by hand survives; the attached tab is parked on a safe
page first, so a profile never drops to zero tabs. A Stop hook sweeps anything
older than ten minutes and `BH_MAX_OWNED` caps mid-run growth, but those are the
backstop, not the plan: the run that opened a tab closes it.

## Checks

- Before a research fan-out: name the profile in every subagent prompt.
- After it: `bhn ls --running` shows one research window, not a column of them.
- After it: `bhn deepsurge owned` is empty, and the user's window is not a wall
  of favicons.
- `bhn profiles` is a listing subcommand, not a profile name.

Related: [[brave-harness-e2e]] for E2E and QA runs, [[browser-native]] for the compact
`bhn` command surface, [[deep-research]] for the lanes that do the fetching.

## Never steal the user's screen (2026-09-29)

The user works in the terminal while agents browse. An agent that raises the browser window switches their screen mid-keystroke.
- **Never call these** in any CDP script: `Page.bringToFront`, `Target.activateTarget`, or the `/json/new` endpoint (it opens a foreground tab). Never set `BH_FOREGROUND` unless the user asked to watch.
- **New tabs:** browser-level `Target.createTarget {url:'about:blank', background:true}`, then `Page.setWebLifecycleState {state:'active'}` on the page session so it renders as if visible. bhn's `open`/`new_tab` already does this.
- **Screenshots:** `~/.claude/skills/ui-craft/scripts/refshot.py`. It was tested with the front app recorded before and after: the old script switched Terminal to Brave, the fixed one left Terminal in front and still captured a full render.
- bhn launches Brave with `--disable-renderer-backgrounding --disable-background-timer-throttling --disable-backgrounding-occluded-windows`, so background tabs paint. A profile launched before this change needs one restart to get the flags; do not restart a profile other agents are using.
